Hello everyone,  

Today, I want to ask you three important questions. Please raise your hand if it applies to you.  

Let’s start with the rightmost row: How many of you browse YouTube to learn a new dish?  
Now, the middle rows: Who watches motivational videos or listens to uplifting songs on YouTube when feeling low?  
And finally, the leftmost row: Who uses AI tools to get advice or opinions?  

Hold onto your curiosity—I’ll come back to these questions later.  

Toastmaster of the Day, fellow Toastmasters, and guests,  

Recently, something thought-provoking happened in my life, and it inspired me to share this speech.  

I work as a team lead at a technology company. For the last three years, I’ve consistently exceeded expectations in my performance appraisals. But despite this, I haven’t been promoted. This time, I decided to have a one-on-one meeting with my manager to discuss my aspirations. My manager’s response wasn’t what I hoped for. He said, “Somnath, promotions don’t happen just because you ask for them. Leadership decides when it feels right.”  

That meeting left me feeling discouraged. I started worrying about my future because I knew I probably wouldn’t get promoted this year either. I wanted to figure out my next step. Naturally, like many of you, I turned to AI tools for advice. I got responses like: “Stay positive and patient” or “Document your achievements and share them with your seniors.” But honestly, none of it felt helpful.  

So, I called my father and told him everything. What he said hit me hard: “Maybe you’re perfect for your current role, but not for the next one. You need to show them that you’re capable of more.”  

I realized he was right. I needed to upskill and demonstrate new abilities. I decided to take online courses, but I struggled to complete them because of my busy schedule. I even tried time management techniques like the Pomodoro Technique and the Eisenhower Matrix, but they didn’t work for me.  

Frustrated, I called my father again. I told him about my struggles, and he asked, “How many leaves do you have?” I replied, “Around 100.” He then said, “Why not take a leave for upskilling?”  

At first, I thought my organization would never approve it. But I decided to try. I sent an email requesting a sabbatical for learning, and while they were reluctant at first, I persisted. Eventually, they approved three months of paid leave! Starting next year, I’ll be on leave to focus on upskilling and completing personal projects.  

Here’s what I learned: Both times, my father gave me unique, out-of-the-box advice. Advice that no AI tool could have provided.  

Now, coming back to my initial questions:  
Before you search for a recipe on YouTube, call your mother or mother-in-law instead. A YouTuber’s recipe might suggest ingredients you don’t have, but your mother will always recommend ones already in your kitchen. Plus, this conversation strengthens your bond with your parents. In today’s busy life, we don’t have many reasons to talk to our parents, so let this be one.  

When you feel low, it’s tempting to browse YouTube for motivational videos or listen to uplifting songs. These might give you a temporary boost, but have you ever thought about reaching out to a close friend or family member instead?

A simple conversation with someone who genuinely cares about you can be far more uplifting than any video. They know you, your strengths, and your struggles. Their words won’t just motivate you—they’ll remind you of your value and help you see things from a fresh perspective. Unlike videos, these interactions build real connections and can leave you feeling truly supported.

